      "claim": "A comprehensive search of Louvre official publications, major travel guides, news archives, and academic literature finds no reference to the pyramid being described as \"unbreakable\" or given a nickname implying indestructibility.",
      "evidence": "Searches of the Louvre website, Lonely Planet, Fodor's, The New York Times, Le Monde, and scholarly articles on the pyramid return only design and aesthetic descriptions, with zero instances of the term \"unbreakable\".",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Because the pyramid is made of glass and steel (materials that can be broken) and there is no credible source labeling it as unbreakable, it is not known for being unbreakable.",
      "evidence": "Combining the material properties (step 2) with the absence of any reputable claim (step 3) leads to the conclusion that the attribute \"unbreakable\" does not apply.",
